<section class="hero">
  <h1>Soli on Cloudflare Workers</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This page was rendered by a Soli app running inside a Cloudflare Worker.
    No server, no container: the Soli interpreter is compiled to WebAssembly
    and boots next to your visitors, in Cloudflare's datacenter
    <strong><%= @runtime["colo"] %></strong>.
  </p>
  <p><a class="button" href="/guide">Deploy your own</a> <a class="button ghost" href="/try">Try it</a></p>
</section>

<section class="grid">
  <article>
    <h2>The same app</h2>
    <p>Routes, controllers, views, layouts and models are the ones <code>soli serve</code> runs.
       <code>soli edge build</code> bundles the source into the Worker; the interpreter loads
       it once per isolate and answers every request through the same request pipeline.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>Small enough</h2>
    <p>The whole interpreter is about 9 MB of WebAssembly, 3 MB compressed. An isolate
       boots the app in a few tens of milliseconds; after that a page takes a few.</p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h2>Database included</h2>
    <p>Models run on Cloudflare D1 — SQLite next to the Worker, no server of your
       own — or on SoliDB over HTTPS. Soli's interpreter is synchronous and both are
       asynchronous: the wasm stack suspends on each query (JavaScript Promise
       Integration) and resumes when the answer arrives.</p>
  </article>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What is on this site</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guide">Guide</a> — from an app folder to a deployed Worker, step by step.</li>
    <li><a href="/limits">Limits</a> — what the edge build runs, and what stays with <code>soli serve</code>.</li>
    <li><a href="/try">Try it</a> — a form whose answer is computed by Soli, here, now.</li>
    <li><a href="/api/info">/api/info</a> — the facts in the footer, as JSON.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
